{"id":"9583ea3b-ce15-4fbc-b5d3-86452fd5428a","object":"chat.completion","created":1784991924,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    min_cluster_size = case[\\\"min_cluster_size\\\"]\\n    clusters_input = case[\\\"clusters\\\"]\\n    parent_map = case[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n    clusters_data = {}\\n    for c in clusters_input:\\n        cid = c[\\\"cluster_id\\\"]\\n        size = c[\\\"size\\\"]\\n        birth = c[\\\"birth_lambda\\\"]\\n        exits = c[\\\"point_exit_lambdas\\\"]\\n        stability = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exits)\\n        clusters_data[cid] = {\\\"size\\\": size, \\\"birth\\\": birth, \\\"exits\\\": exits, \\\"stability\\\": stability}\\n    retained_ids = set(cid for cid, data in clusters_data.items() if data[\\\"size\\\"] >= min_cluster_size)\\n    retained_parent = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        parent = parent_map.get(cid)\\n        while parent is not None and parent not in retained_ids:\\n            parent = parent_map.get(parent)\\n        retained_parent[cid] = parent\\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\\n    root_ids = []\\n    for cid, pid in retained_parent.items():\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_ids.append(cid)\\n        else:\\n            children[pid].append(cid)\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def compute_descendants(node):\\n        if node in descendants:\\n            return descendants[node]\\n        desc = set()\\n        for child in children[node]:\\n            desc.add(child)\\n            desc.update(compute_descendants(child))\\n        descendants[node] = desc\\n        return desc\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        compute_descendants(cid)\\n    clusters_list = [(clusters_data[cid][\\\"stability\\\"], cid) for cid in retained_ids]\\n    clusters_list.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    for stability_val, cid in clusters_list:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        has_selected_desc = False\\n        for desc in descendants[cid]:\\n            if desc in selected:\\n                has_selected_desc = True\\n                break\\n        if has_selected_desc:\\n            continue\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        anc = retained_parent.get(cid)\\n        while anc is not None:\\n            blocked.add(anc)\\n            anc = retained_parent.get(anc)\\n    output_clusters = []\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        data = clusters_data[cid]\\n        output_clusters.append({\\\"cluster_id\\\": cid, \\\"size\\\": data[\\\"size\\\"], \\\"stability\\\": data[\\\"stability\\\"]})\\n    sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\\n    return {\\\"clusters\\\": output_clusters, \\\"selected_cluster_ids\\\": sorted_selected}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for DATA-HDB-02. The task is to condense a hierarchy (given clusters with birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas, and parent mapping) under a min_cluster_size, compute stability for each cluster, and select stable clusters with deterministic tie handling.\n\nInput case payload has:\n- min_cluster_size: integer\n- clusters: list of objects with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list of numbers)\n- parent: mapping from cluster_id to parent cluster_id (or null for root)\n- _case_nonce: not used\n\nOutput must match schema:\n{\n  \"clusters\": list of objects with cluster_id, size, stability (number),\n  \"selected_cluster_ids\": list of strings (ordered)\n}\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n\n1. Validate fields: ensure min_cluster_size is int, clusters list, each cluster has required fields, parent mapping.\n2. Filter out clusters with size < min_cluster_size. These are \"noise\" or removed from condensed tree? According to candidate artifact: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" So we remove them entirely from consideration (not included in output clusters list).\n3. For each retained cluster, compute stability: sum over its points of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Each cluster has \"point_exit_lambdas\" list, one per point. So for each point exit lambda, compute max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) and sum. (Note: the cluster's own size is number of points, and point_exit_lambdas length should equal size? Yes, given in fixture: size=7, point_exit_lambdas length 3? Actually c0 size 7 but point_exit_lambdas length 3. That's inconsistent; but maybe point_exit_lambdas are only for points that exited? The candidate says \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" So presumably each point has an exit lambda; we have point_exit_lambdas list. It might be that exit_lambdas are provided for each point, but length may be less than size? In fixture c0 has size 7 but only 3 exit lambdas. Possibly some points never exit? Then exit_lambda is infinite? But max(0, inf - birth) would be inf. That seems off. Or maybe only points that exited at finite lambda are listed, others persist? The candidate says \"exit_lambda - birth_lambda\" and we only sum over points. Could be that we only consider points that have an exit lambda? If a point never exits, its stability contribution is zero? However, the description \"sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points\" implies all points. In given fixtures, it seems point_exit_lambdas length is not equal to size (c0 size 7, exit lambdas 3; c1 size 4, exit lambdas 3; c2 size 2, exit lambdas 1). This mismatch suggests that point_exit_lambdas are only the lambdas at which points exited within the cluster's lifetime, maybe only those that exited before the cluster's parent? Actually in HDBSCAN condensed tree, each point in a cluster has a lambda where it exits the cluster (either into a child cluster or as noise). So each point has exactly one exit lambda (or infinity if it never exits). In the fixtures, they have less exit lambdas than size, which is odd. Possibly the list includes only distinct exit lambdas? Or maybe the size includes points from children? But the problem says \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" So we need to know each point's exit lambda. Given that fixtures are incomplete, maybe we should assume that the point_exit_lambdas array contains the exit lambdas for all points, but the size is the total number of points in that cluster (including those that were inherited from children?). Actually in condensed tree, a cluster's size is the number of points that are assigned to that cluster at its birth lambda (i.e., points that have not yet exited). As lambda increases, points exit. The point_exit_lambdas list gives the lambda at which each point exits. The length of point_exit_lambdas should equal the size, because each point has an exit lambda (could be infinity but not listed). In the fixtures, lengths are less than size. That is a problem. Maybe the fixtures are not representative of all cases, but the implementation must handle any well-formed input. Possibly the definition is: point_exit_lambdas are the exit lambdas for points that actually exit within the hierarchy (i.e., points that do not survive to infinite lambda). Points that persist have no exit lambda recorded? Then stability sum uses only those with finite exit? That would be ambiguous.\n\nGiven the candidate artifact: \"stability_accounting\" property suggests standard HDBSCAN stability: stability = sum over points (lambda_max - lambda_min) where lambda_min is birth of cluster, lambda_max is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (either by moving to a child or becoming noise). In HDBSCAN, each point has a lambda value when it \"leaves\" the cluster (or infinity). So point_exit_lambdas should be complete. The fixture might be simplified but we must implement accordingly. I'll assume that point_exit_lambdas array contains the exit lambda for each point in the cluster (so length = size). If not, we may have to handle missing data? But the task says to validate required fields. The required fields in the cluster include size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. So we can assume length equals size. In the fixtures, it's not, but those may be test data only. We'll follow the assumption.\n\nThus compute stability for each cluster:\nstability = sum( max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for exit_lambda in point_exit_lambdas )\n\nNote: exit_lambda could be less than birth_lambda? Probably not, but we use max(0, ...).\n\n4. Build tree structure: parent mapping. We need to determine descendant relationships. For clusters that are retained (after filtering size), we need to select stable clusters such that if a cluster is selected, no descendant is selected, and ancestors are blocked.\n\nAlgorithm for selection:\n- Sort clusters by stability descending, then by cluster_id ascending (alphabetically? likely string comparison).\n- Iterate through sorted list; maintain a set of blocked clusters (ancestors of selected clusters). Also maintain a set of selected cluster ids.\n- For each cluster in sorted order:\n  - If it is not in blocked set and no descendant of it is already selected? Actually \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So we need to ensure that if a descendant is already selected, we cannot select this ancestor. Similarly, if this cluster has an ancestor that is already selected? The wording: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" meaning if there is a selected descendant, then this cluster (ancestor) is blocked. Also \"then block its ancestors\" means after selection, mark its ancestors as blocked so they can't be selected later.\n\nBut also, if a descendant is selected, then ancestor is blocked. So we can precompute the descendant relationships. We'll need a mapping from cluster to its descendants (possibly recursive). Since hierarchy is tree (parent mapping, parent is null for root). We'll need to handle that clusters that were dropped (size < min) are not part of the tree? The condensed tree should only include retained clusters. However, the parent mapping may refer to dropped clusters. The candidate says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" So they are removed from the tree entirely. Then for remaining clusters, we need to adjust the tree: if a parent is dropped, then the child becomes a child of the parent's parent? Actually in condensed tree construction, you prune clusters below min_cluster_size: they are \"absorbed\" into their parent. But the candidate says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" and then we only compute stability for retained clusters. But for the selection algorithm, we need the hierarchy among retained clusters. The typical HDBSCAN condensed tree reconstruction: clusters with size < min_cluster_size are removed and their points are reassigned to the parent cluster. However, here we are not reassigning points; we are just selecting clusters from the condensed tree after pruning. The output clusters are only those retained (size >= min_cluster_size). They keep their original parent relationships? But if a parent is dropped, the child's parent becomes the nearest ancestor that is retained. We need to reflect that. The algorithm: after dropping, for each retained cluster, find the lowest retained ancestor (including itself). That defines the tree for selection.\n\nAlternative: The candidate says \"Condensed-tree construction\" and \"drop clusters smaller than min cluster size\". In typical HDBSCAN condensed tree, you remove clusters below min cluster size and then update the tree: the children of a removed cluster become children of the nearest larger ancestor. So we must implement that.\n\nSteps for condensed tree construction:\n- Start with all clusters.\n- Compute stability for all clusters? Or only for those we will retain? Stability is needed for retained clusters only, but we may need stability for selection.\n- Filter out clusters with size < min_cluster_size. Those are \"noise\" (or absorbed).\n- For the remaining clusters, we need to build the hierarchy: the parent is the nearest retained ancestor. We can do that by traversing from each cluster up the original parent chain until we find a retained cluster (or root). If root is dropped, then it becomes root? Actually if all ancestors are dropped, then it becomes a new root.\n\nSo:\n- Build a set of retained cluster ids (size >= min_cluster_size).\n- Create a mapping from cluster_id to its retained parent: for each retained cluster, walk up original parent chain until we find a retained cluster or reach None (original root). If we find None or a dropped cluster that has no retained ancestor? If original root is dropped, then the retained cluster becomes a root (parent None). So we set its parent to the first retained ancestor, or None if none.\n- Also we need to ensure tree is a forest; but typically there is one root.\n\nNow we have a tree of retained clusters. The stability for each is computed as above.\n\nSelection algorithm on this tree:\n- We want to select clusters locally maximizing stability, such that if a cluster is selected, no descendant is selected, and it blocks ancestors (i.e., no ancestor can be selected). This is similar to selecting a set of non-overlapping branches with highest stability. In HDBSCAN, they do: sort clusters by stability (and cluster ID ties), then iterate: for each cluster, if it has no selected descendant (i.e., all its descendants are not selected), then select it and block its ancestors. The blocking of ancestors is implicit because we only check descendants: if we select a cluster, we mark all its ancestors as blocked (they cannot be selected later). So we need to maintain a set of blocked clusters. Alternatively, we could compute for each cluster the sum of stabilities of its children vs its own stability; but the iteration approach is simpler.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use a recursive function or iterative DFS to get descendants of each cluster. Since tree is small, we can precompute descendants set for each cluster.\n- In selection loop:\n  - For each cluster in sorted order:\n    - If cluster_id in blocked: continue.\n    - Check if any of its descendants are already selected. If yes, skip (this cluster is blocked by its descendant). If no, select it, then add all its ancestors to blocked set.\n- After processing, we have selected_cluster_ids. They should be sorted? Output schema: selected_cluster_ids is an array of strings. The order: the candidate says \"sorted selected IDs\". So we need to sort them, likely alphabetically (by cluster_id). We'll sort ascending.\n\nReturn output dict with \"clusters\" list (each with cluster_id, size, stability) and \"selected_cluster_ids\" sorted.\n\nEdge cases:\n- If no clusters retained, return empty clusters list and empty selected list.\n- Stability is a number (float? integer? The schema says \"type\": \"number\", could be integer or float. We'll compute as float.\n- Validate input: ensure all required fields exist. If not, maybe raise? But function must not fail; assume input is valid.\n\nImplementation plan in Python:\ndef solve(case):\n    min_cluster_size = case[\"min_cluster_size\"]\n    clusters_input = case[\"clusters\"]\n    parent_map = case[\"parent\"]\n\n    # Validate (optional but safe)\n    # ...\n\n    # Build dict of cluster data\n    clusters_data = {}\n    for c in clusters_input:\n        cid = c[\"cluster_id\"]\n        size = c[\"size\"]\n        birth = c[\"birth_lambda\"]\n        exits = c[\"point_exit_lambdas\"]\n        # Compute stability: sum over exits max(0, exit - birth)\n        stability = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exits)\n        clusters_data[cid] = {\n            \"size\": size,\n            \"birth\": birth,\n            \"exits\": exits,\n            \"stability\": stability\n        }\n\n    # Filter by min_cluster_size\n    retained_ids = set(cid for cid, data in clusters_data.items() if data[\"size\"] >= min_cluster_size)\n\n    # Build retained parent mapping\n    retained_parent = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        parent = parent_map.get(cid)\n        while parent is not None and parent not in retained_ids:\n            parent = parent_map.get(parent)  # may go to None\n        retained_parent[cid] = parent  # None if no retained ancestor\n\n    # Build tree structure: children mapping\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\n    root_ids = []\n    for cid, pid in retained_parent.items():\n        if pid is None:\n            root_ids.append(cid)\n        else:\n            children[pid].append(cid)\n\n    # Precompute descendants for each retained cluster (including itself?) For selection we need to check if any descendant is selected. We'll compute all descendant ids recursively.\n    descendants = {}\n    def compute_descendants(node):\n        if node in descendants:\n            return descendants[node]\n        desc = set()\n        for child in children[node]:\n            desc.add(child)\n            desc.update(compute_descendants(child))\n        descendants[node] = desc\n        return desc\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        compute_descendants(cid)\n\n    # Compute sorted list of clusters (by stability descending, then cluster_id ascending)\n    clusters_list = [(clusters_data[cid][\"stability\"], cid) for cid in retained_ids]\n    clusters_list.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))  # descending stability, ascending id\n\n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n\n    for stability_val, cid in clusters_list:\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant is selected\n        has_selected_desc = False\n        for desc in descendants[cid]:\n            if desc in selected:\n                has_selected_desc = True\n                break\n        if has_selected_desc:\n            continue\n        # Select this cluster\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # Block ancestors\n        # Walk up retained parent chain and add to blocked\n        anc = retained_parent.get(cid)\n        while anc is not None:\n            blocked.add(anc)\n            anc = retained_parent.get(anc)\n\n    # Build output clusters list\n    output_clusters = []\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        data = clusters_data[cid]\n        output_clusters.append({\n            \"cluster_id\": cid,\n            \"size\": data[\"size\"],\n            \"stability\": data[\"stability\"]  # could be int or float\n        })\n\n    # Sort selected cluster ids\n    sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\n\n    return {\n        \"clusters\": output_clusters,\n        \"selected_cluster_ids\": sorted_selected\n    }\n\nThis should satisfy.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with a single key \"implementation\" whose value is the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nWe need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the code as a single line? No, we can include newlines escaped as \\n. But careful with indentation.\n\nBetter to produce a multi-line string in JSON using \\n. We'll write the code cleanly and then escape.\n\nWe'll need to ensure no backticks or external text.\n\nWe'll output exactly:\n{\"implementation\":\"<escaped Python code>\"}\n\nLet's produce the Python code string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    min_cluster_size = case[\"min_cluster_size\"]\n    clusters_input = case[\"clusters\"]\n    parent_map = case[\"parent\"]\n    clusters_data = {}\n    for c in clusters_input:\n        cid = c[\"cluster_id\"]\n        size = c[\"size\"]\n        birth = c[\"birth_lambda\"]\n        exits = c[\"point_exit_lambdas\"]\n        stability = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exits)\n        clusters_data[cid] = {\"size\": size, \"birth\": birth, \"exits\": exits, \"stability\": stability}\n    retained_ids = set(cid for cid, data in clusters_data.items() if data[\"size\"] >= min_cluster_size)\n    retained_parent = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        parent = parent_map.get(cid)\n        while parent is not None and parent not in retained_ids:\n            parent = parent_map.get(parent)\n        retained_parent[cid] = parent\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\n    root_ids = []\n    for cid, pid in retained_parent.items():\n        if pid is None:\n            root_ids.append(cid)\n        else:\n            children[pid].append(cid)\n    descendants = {}\n    def compute_descendants(node):\n        if node in descendants:\n            return descendants[node]\n        desc = set()\n        for child in children[node]:\n            desc.add(child)\n            desc.update(compute_descendants(child))\n        descendants[node] = desc\n        return desc\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        compute_descendants(cid)\n    clusters_list = [(clusters_data[cid][\"stability\"], cid) for cid in retained_ids]\n    clusters_list.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n    for stability_val, cid in clusters_list:\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        has_selected_desc = False\n        for desc in descendants[cid]:\n            if desc in selected:\n                has_selected_desc = True\n                break\n        if has_selected_desc:\n            continue\n        selected.add(cid)\n        anc = retained_parent.get(cid)\n        while anc is not None:\n            blocked.add(anc)\n            anc = retained_parent.get(anc)\n    output_clusters = []\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        data = clusters_data[cid]\n        output_clusters.append({\"cluster_id\": cid, \"size\": data[\"size\"], \"stability\": data[\"stability\"]})\n    sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\n    return {\"clusters\": output_clusters, \"selected_cluster_ids\": sorted_selected}\n\nNow escape for JSON: need to replace backslashes with double backslashes, double quotes with backslash+quote, newlines with \\n.\n\nI'll produce the JSON string manually.\n\nMake sure to include the function exactly. Also note that we don't import anything, but we use built-in functions.\n\nPotential issue: The \"descendants\" function may be recursive; that's fine.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1609,"completion_tokens":4903,"total_tokens":6512,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4226},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":73},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}